            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Link Groups",
              "url": "https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnorlink.pdf",
              "locator": "§2 and Lemma 2, PDF pp. 2–3"
            },
            {
              "title": "Ryan Stees, Milnor’s Invariants for Knots and Links in Closed Orientable 3-Manifolds",
              "url": "https://scholarworks.iu.edu/iuswrrest/api/core/bitstreams/54c7d68f-d982-4e1f-9550-e54140ada5ea/content",
              "locator": "§2.5, printed pp. 16–18 (PDF pp. 24–26)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Use integral homology. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ be a nonempty labeled oriented smooth link in $S^3$ with chosen pairwise disjoint product tubes and exterior $E_L$. Then $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$, with the positive meridians as its ordered basis. Each boundary torus $T_i$ has a product basis consisting of its positive meridian and a degree-one section. There is a unique degree-one class on $T_i$ whose image has zero $i$th meridian coordinate; this class is primitive and has a simple closed representative.\n\nLet $\\tau$ be an $r$-component string link with chosen disjoint product tubes in $D^2\\times[0,1]$, exterior $X_\\tau$, and bottom and top punctured disks $P_-$ and $P_+$. Then each $\\pi_1(P_\\pm)$ is free of rank $r$. Positive endpoint meridians with the compatible accesses transported from a point-punctured-disk spine form an ordered free basis. For arbitrary independently chosen access paths, the corresponding meridian is $c_i m_i c_i^{-1}$, where $m_i$ is the compatible-stem meridian and $c_i$ is the recorded access-path difference; these conjugates need not be a free basis. In particular $H_1(P_\\pm;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$, and $H_2(P_\\pm;\\mathbb Z)=0$. The exterior satisfies $H_1(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$ and $H_2(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)=0$. Each end inclusion sends its ordered positive meridian basis to the ordered positive meridian basis of $H_1(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)$. Consequently both induced homomorphisms on $H_1$ are isomorphisms and both induced homomorphisms on $H_2$ are surjective.",
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "def-topological-linking-number-by-meridian-coordinate",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Topological linking number as a meridian coordinate",
        "deps": [
          "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
          "def-knot-exterior",
          "lem-string-link-exterior-homology-and-meridian-maps"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Link Groups",
              "url": "https://webhomes.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnorlink.pdf",
              "locator": "§§1–2, PDF pp. 1–6, positive meridians and link-group conventions"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $K$ and $J$ be disjoint oriented smooth embedded circles in the oriented\nthree-sphere. Choose a closed product tube $N(K)$ disjoint from $J$, let\n$E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$, and let $\\mu_K$ be the positive\nmeridian. Since $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z[\\mu_K]$, define the topological\nlinking number relative to this tube by the unique integer $a$ satisfying\n$$[J]=a[\\mu_K]\\quad\\text{in }H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe chosen tube is part of the data for this definition.",
        "justified_by": []
